//! **THE /dev/uart0 PROTOCOL** (sawos design 21, M4 unit 5 — the money shot).
//!
//! A UART driver that serves its device over a pipe needs a WIRE, and this is
//! it: one op byte, then payload. Nothing here is kernel surface — the brief
//! forbids growing `kernel/abi` or `sos` for this unit, and the reason is the
//! design rather than the scope. A device protocol belongs to the device's
//! driver and its clients; what the KERNEL supplies is the connection, the
//! message and the one-shot reply pair, and it supplies exactly those to every
//! protocol equally. M5's namespace is what will let a client find this service
//! by a PATH instead of by a boot tag.
//!
//! **WHY IT IS A PACKAGE AND NOT A COMMENT.** Three programs compile against
//! these numbers — the driver, the client child, and each root launcher acting
//! as a client of its own — and the whole claim of the unit is that they AGREE.
//! A copy per program is a copy that can drift, and the drift would show up as
//! a wrong byte rather than as a compile error. This is `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`'
//! argument at one altitude down: the two halves that must agree compile
//! against one declaration. It sits in `tests/` because it is harness
//! vocabulary, and NOT in `sosrt` because that package is the SOS runtime —
//! arch-free and role-free code every image links — and a device protocol is
//! neither.
//!
//! # The wire
//!
//! ```text
//! byte 0      the op                    (UartOp, a raw-backed enum)
//! bytes 1..   the payload               (`len - 1` of them)
//! ```
//!
//! and the four ops, each with its OWN kernel-level shape:
//!
//!   - `Read`  — a CALL. The reply carries the next input byte, and the reply's
//!               own `len` is what says how many. If no input is pending the
//!               driver HOLDS the obligation and answers when its IRQ delivers
//!               one; a fused `send` PARKS meanwhile, so a blocking read is
//!               composed entirely out of shipped parts and the kernel learns
//!               nothing about UARTs.
//!   - `Write` — a TELL: post, drop the claim, never look back (`designs/010`
//!               ruling 4). The driver writes the payload out its window.
//!   - `Sync`  — a CALL with an empty reply. A connection's ring is FIFO, so an
//!               answered `Sync` proves every message posted BEFORE it has been
//!               served — which is how a client that only TELLs learns when its
//!               writes reached the device.
//!   - `Mark`  — a CALL with an empty reply, and the one op here that is pure
//!               instrumentation: it tells the driver to take a trap reading, so
//!               the serve loop's cost can be measured over a window the case
//!               controls. It carries no authority and reads no device.
//!
//! # Cancellation is not an op
//!
//! There is no `Cancel` byte, deliberately. A client that gives up a `Read`
//! DROPS ITS CLAIM — §2.1's ratified cancellation primitive — and the driver
//! learns about it through the request's own ABANDONED level, which it is
//! already watching because it is holding the obligation. Putting a cancel on
//! the wire would need the driver to correlate two messages and would race the
//! reply it is trying to cancel; the reference count does not.

import sos.{PipeMsg, PIPE_BODY_BYTES}
import sosrt.{ascii_byte}

/// The op byte. RAW-BACKED because it crosses a wire (design 145 B2, and the
/// house rule that a closed named set of values is a backed enum rather than a
/// family of statics): the values are ABI between the driver and its clients,
/// so every case states its own and a reorder cannot renumber them.
public enum UartOp: UInt8 {
    case Read = 1,
    case Write = 2,
    case Sync = 3,
    case Mark = 4,
}

extension UartOp {
    /// What a transcript should call this op.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Read -> "read",
            case Write -> "write",
            case Sync -> "sync",
            case Mark -> "mark",
        }
    }
}

/// Where the op byte sits, and where the payload starts.
public static OP_BYTE: Int = 0
public static PAYLOAD_BYTE: Int = 1

/// The most payload one message can carry: the body minus the op byte.
public static MAX_PAYLOAD: Int = PIPE_BODY_BYTES - 1

static_assert(MAX_PAYLOAD > 0, "a message must have room for an op and a byte")

/// **THE REQUEST BUFFER, AND IT IS A STRUCT FOR A LANGUAGE REASON.** The body
/// every send takes is a `&[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]`, so a builder wants to
/// write one through a `&var` — and a `&var [T; N]` PARAMETER's elements are not
/// assignable in sawc today (SL-13), while the same array reached as a field of
/// a `&var` struct is. One field, no ceremony, and `&buf.bytes` is what goes to
/// the wire.
public struct UartBody {
    public bytes: [UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
}

extension UartBody {
    /// A zeroed body.
    public static func empty() -> UartBody {
        UartBody(bytes: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    }

    /// An op and nothing else. Answers the message's `len`.
    public func bare(&var self, op: UartOp) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[OP_BYTE] = op as UInt8
        1
    }

    /// An op and one payload byte. Answers the message's `len`.
    public func one(&var self, op: UartOp, b: UInt8) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[OP_BYTE] = op as UInt8
        self.bytes[PAYLOAD_BYTE] = b
        2
    }

    /// **A REPLY carrying one byte.** Answers the reply's `len`.
    ///
    /// A REPLY HAS NO OP BYTE, and that is the protocol rather than an
    /// omission: a reply answers exactly one request, so the request's op is
    /// what says how to read it, and the reply's own `len` is the length prefix
    /// §2.1 already gives every message. A second framing would be a second
    /// thing to keep in step.
    public func answer(&var self, b: UInt8) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[0] = b
        1
    }

    /// **A REPLY carrying nothing.** Answers `0`, which §2.1 allows and which
    /// reads no buffer at all — the acknowledgement `Sync` and `Mark` want.
    public func nothing(&self) -> UInt { 0 }

    /// An op and a line of ASCII. Answers the message's `len`.
    ///
    /// A scalar that is not ASCII becomes `?`, which is `ascii_byte`'s rule and
    /// the kernel console's — so a line written through a driver's window reads
    /// the same as one written through a System op. Text longer than
    /// `MAX_PAYLOAD` is CUT rather than wrapped: the maximum is published, the
    /// caller chose the string, and a silently split message would be two
    /// requests where the caller wrote one.
    public func text(&var self, op: UartOp, s: String) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[OP_BYTE] = op as UInt8
        var n: Int = 0
        for scalar in s.chars() {
            if n < MAX_PAYLOAD {
                self.bytes[PAYLOAD_BYTE + n] = ascii_byte(scalar)
                n = n + 1
            }
        }
        (n + 1) as UInt
    }
}

/// **THE OP A RECEIVED MESSAGE NAMES, OR `None` FOR A BYTE THAT NAMES NO OP.**
///
/// The Optional is load-bearing rather than a transient (the Sep-2 discipline):
/// a request arrives from a process the driver does not trust, so "this byte is
/// not one of my ops" is a REAL DOMAIN VALUE at a protocol boundary, and it is
/// what `from(raw:)` exists for — an unrecognized wire byte is data, never a
/// trap. There is no error channel to put it on: this is a decode, not an
/// operation that can fail.
///
/// A message with no bytes at all (`len == 0`, which §2.1 allows) names no op
/// either, and the same `None` says so.
public func op_of(msg: &PipeMsg) -> UartOp? {
    if msg.len == 0 { return None }
    UartOp.from(raw: msg.bytes[OP_BYTE])
}

/// How many payload bytes a received message carries.
public func payload_len(msg: &PipeMsg) -> UInt {
    if msg.len == 0 { return 0 }
    msg.len - 1
}

/// Payload byte `i` of a received message. Out of range answers zero rather
/// than panicking: the sender chose the length and the receiver is a SERVER,
/// which must not be endable by a client's arithmetic.
public func payload_byte(msg: &PipeMsg, i: Int) -> UInt8 {
    if i < 0 || (i as UInt) >= payload_len(&msg) { return 0 }
    msg.bytes[PAYLOAD_BYTE + i]
}
